+ {"id": "HARDEN-DUCKBRAIN-003", "title": "[P1] Hermes memory provider went 'unavailable' because /health could be rate-limited — 127.0.0.1 is one shared token bucket for every local caller", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 2, "created_at": "2026-11-04T00:16:56Z", "reasoning": "Bane reported 2026-11-03: 'Memory provider duckbrain is selected but reports unavailable — external memory is disabled for this session'. Root cause measured, not guessed: (1) the daemon on :3000 runs --rate-limit 600 as a PER-IP token bucket (src/auth/ratelimit.ts), and every caller on this host — fleet agents, cron, MCP servers, supervisors, probes — shares 127.0.0.1; (2) /health was registered AFTER rateLimitMiddleware (src/cli/http.ts:688 vs :564), so it was itself rate-limited; (3) ten sequential unauthenticated probes returned 8x 200 (2.6ms) and 2x 429 (0.6ms) while the process was healthy; (4) the provider's is_available() required exactly r.status == 200, so one throttled probe flipped the whole session to 'external memory disabled'. /metrics escaped this only because OBSDB-001 mounted it above the limiter. The systemic half is not fixed by the route move: a single burst from ONE agent can still throttle every other local caller's API traffic, which is why this row stays open after the liveness fix.", "acceptance_criteria": "Two halves. (A) LIVENESS — landed 121aa62c: /health is registered with /metrics above the limiter; a new suite proves 25 /health probes and 25 /metrics scrapes at rateLimit=2 yield zero 429s while /stats still 429s with Retry-After (and the test fails with the fix stashed). The provider (~/.hermes/plugins/duckbrain) now treats 429/503 from a live socket as ALIVE and only 401/403/no-token/refused as unavailable, proven against local servers for 200/429/503/401/403/refused. Verify live AFTER the daemon restart: 25 back-to-back curl /health all answer 200 or 503, never 429. (B) SYSTEMIC (still open) — local callers must not share one throttle bucket in a way that lets one agent starve the rest: either exempt loopback/liveness-class routes explicitly with a documented list, or split the bucket (per-principal or per-socket class), or raise the local budget with a measured basis. Prove with a transcript: drive 200 rapid requests from one local caller and show another local caller's /api/memories still answers 200 (i.e. one caller cannot starve another). Do not simply raise the limit without the second-caller proof.", "updated_at": "2026-11-04T00:54:05Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "d148d11072b6a4c3934b30d571fa060287252946c4483fce9496666be0848851"}, "worker_status": "pending", "commit_hash": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "foreman_note": "LIVENESS HALF DONE 121aa62c, but it went RED on CI (run 41745657088, 3 failures) and the repair is 37f81e19: (a) tests/http-ratelimit.int.test.ts hammered /health as the cheapest URL, so an exempt /health produced no 429 and the X-RateLimit-Limit assertion read a 503 — they now hammer /api/namespaces, treating 401 as 'under the limit' because the limiter runs before auth; (b) tests/ratelimit-squatter.int.test.ts had depended on draining A's /health bucket so the readiness poll saw 429 from the squatter — with /health exempt, A answers 200 instantly and waitForUrl correctly read a 200 from a daemon it never started, so the test now waits for B to actually lose the bind race before asking the guard (same shape, made explicit instead of accidental). Both green locally under vitest.integration.config.ts (6 tests). PROCESS ERROR WORTH RECORDING: I ran targeted suites and reported success, and CI caught what they missed — twice (the second: OBSDB-002 grew the family set to eight and OBSDB-001's exact-shape test went red, fixed in 9a4aeb57). CI is GREEN on 9a4aeb57. Local full-suite runs still show ~10 contention failures in ddl-* and memories-search suites that all pass in isolation — the known load flakes, NOT fixed here. (B) SYSTEMIC still open: one local caller can still starve another's API traffic.\n\nMEASURED BASIS FOR THE LIMIT RAISE (Bane: we will raise it soon). Ledger census over 942,228 rows, 2026-10-07T12:10Z..2026-11-04T00:53Z: 429s RECORDED IN THE LEDGER = 0, and that zero is the blind spot itself — the limiter runs before the read ledger, so a refusal cannot be recorded there. It is not evidence of no refusals: ten sequential /health probes on the live daemon produced 2x429 while the ledger shows none. Sustained rate: 7d peak 759/min, p99 532/min, p50 3/min, 56 of 7,362 active minutes (0.76%) above the 600 cap, ZERO minutes above 1000; 24h peak 693/min, p99 566/min, 9 minutes (0.89%) above 600. Bucket shape (src/auth/ratelimit.ts:90): maxTokens = burstSize ?? requestsPerMinute = 600, refill 600/60000 = 0.01 tokens/ms = 10 req/s — so sustained demand above 10/s drains the bucket and the LAST callers of that minute get 429 regardless of their own rate. That matches the observed peak (693/min ~ 11.5/s for a full minute). RECOMMENDATION with a basis: requestsPerMinute 600 -> 1200 (never exceeded in 7d, 2x headroom) and set burstSize explicitly to 300 (so a single agent's burst cannot empty a bucket sized for the whole fleet); then re-measure rejected_total{reason=rate_limit} for 48h and only raise again if it is still non-zero. Do NOT raise without OBSDB-002 live: before it, a raise is unverifiable because refusals were invisible."}
+ {"id": "HARDEN-DUCKBRAIN-004", "title": "[P2] Two lockfiles must be updated in lockstep and nothing reminds you — adding prom-client broke CI twice today", "status": "pending", "priority": "P2", "complexity": 2, "created_at": "2026-11-04T01:05:42Z", "reasoning": "The repo tracks BOTH pnpm-lock.yaml (CI installs with 'pnpm install --frozen-lockfile', .github/workflows/ci.yml:35) and package-lock.json (the Dockerfile installs with 'npm ci', Dockerfile:8). Adding prom-client in OBSDB-001 updated only pnpm, so src/test/lockfile-sync.test.ts failed the first full suite and then the CI run; the repair (90e20a0e) regenerated package-lock.json with 'npm install --package-lock-only --ignore-scripts' (+39 lines). The same class of drift is one dependency away from happening again and the reminder is a test that fails 4 minutes into a suite rather than a step that cannot be skipped. Not urgent, not cosmetic: a red main blocks every worker behind it.", "acceptance_criteria": "Make the drift impossible or immediately loud, prove it, and do not remove the npm lockfile without an owner decision (the Dockerfile depends on it). Options, pick and prove ONE: (A) a pre-commit/CI step that runs both 'pnpm install --lockfile-only' and 'npm install --package-lock-only --ignore-scripts' whenever package.json changes, and fails with a diff if either lockfile is dirty — prove by adding a throwaway dependency and showing the gate fires BEFORE the test suite; (B) a single source of truth (drop one lockfile and the install path that needs it), which requires an owner decision and a Dockerfile change — prove with a green docker build leg. Either way: record the chosen rule in the contributing docs, and show 'pnpm test:run' plus the docker leg green on the change.", "updated_at": "2026-11-04T01:05:42Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "0b50a4ab40a0a11c059be5c38b333dd12eb0b6cc9960667b431901e21d2bb71a"}, "worker_status": "pending", "commit_hash": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "foreman_note": null}
+ {"id": "PERF-DUCKBRAIN-001", "title": "[P1] 11% of /api/memories reads take >=3s and the cause is FILE COUNT (20k-29k jsonl files per namespace), not embeddings", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 3, "created_at": "2026-11-04T01:10:15Z", "reasoning": "Measured against the live daemon 2026-11-04 (read-only, authenticated). Ledger census over 52,139 GET /api/memories calls: 55.7% under 300ms (median 73ms), 20.1% in 1-3s, 8.6% in 3-10s, 2.9% over 10s -> 11.45% at >=3s, and 5,968 of those slow calls carried NO query string, so this is the plain LIST path, not semantic search. My first hypothesis (embedding in the path) was wrong. Direct probes isolate the real variable: namespace 'hermes' (6 jsonl files, 145 rows, 5.6M) answers limit=10 in 32ms and limit=200 in 33ms; 'coding-hermes' (20,054 files, 494,973 rows, 2.3G) answers limit=10 in 1.42-2.00s across three repeats; 'fleet-quality' (29,080 files, 94,483 rows, 346M) took 13.26s cold then 1.82s warm. Latency tracks FILE COUNT, not row count and not payload (fleet-quality returns 2.05MB for limit=200 yet beats a 1.4s limit=10 on file-dense coding-hermes). Two consequences: the tail is a per-read directory-walk/metadata cost that no cache can hide on the first call, and 'limit' buys nothing because selection still has to visit the files.", "acceptance_criteria": "Cut the tail with a mechanism, not a cache. (1) Reproduce: a script that, for each of coding-hermes and fleet-quality, runs limit=10 three times cold and reports median vs the file count — committed so the numbers are re-derivable, not quoted. (2) Fix the read path so a limit=10 list does not depend on total file count: compact/merge the namespace's small jsonl files (there is already squash/compaction machinery — prove which one applies) and/or add a bounded index (e.g. newest-N by mtime/segment ordering) that the list endpoint consults before opening files. (3) Prove the effect with the same script: cold limit=10 on both namespaces under 500ms, and no row lost — compare namespaces prune --dry-run plus per-namespace row counts before/after, and confirm GET /api/memories?namespace=X&limit=200 returns the same first-200 newest rows as before the change. (4) Show the ledger's own p95 for GET /api/memories falling after the fix (or state honestly that the window is too short to move p95 yet and give a sampled measurement instead). Do NOT simply raise caches or add a bigger timeout.", "updated_at": "2026-11-04T01:13:37Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "662b29f55d17e638d0a20330321b51bc441043ac924a892cf6e2b22b6c7010ab"}, "worker_status": "pending", "commit_hash": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "foreman_note": "PROOF, PART 1 (compaction) — done on the COPY /home/kara/.duckbrain-live/namespaces, production untouched. 'duckbrain squash --segments --partition=config/2026-08 --namespace=coding-hermes' took the partition 10,000 segments -> 22 (dry-run) and 10,001 files -> 23 on disk; event/2026-08 went 9,798 -> 44. RECORDS PRESERVED: production config/2026-08 = 113,311 rows across 10,001 files; compacted copy = 113,311 rows across 23 files (ROWS MATCH). LATENCY, same machine, same daemon code, --auth=none so no token can distort the read (script /home/kara/clock/prove-perf001-compaction.sh, port 28701): production (fragmented) limit=10 was 1.42/1.88/2.00s across three runs; compacted copy limit=10 is 0.98s cold then 0.47/0.50s warm, and limit=200 returns 1.04MB in 0.51s. So file-count compaction is worth ~3-4x and is the cheap half. IT IS NOT THE WHOLE FIX: the compacted tree still holds ~300 files across partitions (event/2026-09 = 231, plus 23 + 44 + audit) and a limit=10 read still pays ~0.47s, which is consistent with the endpoint opening every partition of the domain and sorting before it slices — i.e. 'limit' still buys nothing. That is the read-path half of the acceptance criteria and is UNSTARTED. Honest state: mechanism identified, 3-4x proven on a copy, target (cold <500ms) NOT met yet, production NOT compacted (owner-gated: it rewrites live memory files).\n\nPRE-FLIGHT FOUND (before anyone compacts production): S3 state is CENTRAL at namespaces/.s3state (not per-namespace), and the CLI already ships 'duckbrain s3 ghosts' + 's3 clear'. Compaction deletes ~10k local files per partition, so the S3 mirror will hold ghost objects for every segment that disappeared — harmless for reads, but it means (a) a naive 'pull' could restore the fragmented layout and undo the compaction, and (b) storage accounting will overstate the namespace. So the production run MUST be: squash --segments (per partition) -> 'duckbrain s3 status <ns>' -> 'duckbrain s3 ghosts <ns>' to enumerate what disappeared -> 's3 clear' (or an explicit sync) so the mirror matches the compacted tree -> re-run 's3 status' to prove convergence. Also confirmed: namespaces/ is INSIDE the duckbrain git repo but gitignored (.gitignore:13), so compaction creates NO git churn — the risk is entirely in the S3 mirror, not git. This pre-flight is not yet exercised end to end; it is written down so the production step cannot be done half-way.\n\nFLEET CENSUS (2026-11-04): the fragmentation is not two namespaces, it is six, and they are 98.7% of every jsonl file the substrate holds. Total across 291 namespaces = 387,175 jsonl files. Top: scheduler 258,578 | pokemon-global 49,622 | fleet-quality 29,080 | coding-hermes 20,054 | hermes-telemetry 14,800 | relationships 10,010 | skills 1,758 | hermes-memory 798 | chat-archive 605 | task-router 46 | routing 40 | default 28. So the production compaction work list is 6 namespaces (~382k of 387k files), not the 2 measured in part 1. Note relationships (10,010 files) is the PERSONAL memory namespace — the one behind Bane's own assistant memory — so the tail is not only a fleet-throughput issue. scheduler at 258,578 files is 67% of the entire substrate by file count and was not in the original measurement at all. Consequence for the acceptance criteria: scale the proof per namespace (rows preserved, latency before/after) rather than declaring victory on coding-hermes alone, and expect the scheduler namespace to dominate the wall-clock of any compaction run."}
++{"id": "SUPA9-PK-FILTER-001", "title": "[P2] GET ignores ?pk=eq.N while PATCH/DELETE require it — the same address means 'one row' to a write verb and 'all rows' to a read", "status": "pending", "priority": "P2", "complexity": 2, "created_at": "2026-11-04T02:30:55Z", "reasoning": "Found by driving the declared-table REST surface end to end on a scratch daemon 2026-11-04 (/home/kara/clock/prove-supa-surface.sh, port 28801/28802, table 'widgets' with primary key id). PATCH and DELETE address rows with ?pk=eq.<value> and both work ({\"updated\":1}, {\"deleted\":1}); a GET with the SAME ?pk=eq.1 returns EVERY row in the table. It is not a general 'unknown filter is ignored' policy either: ?bogus=eq.9 is correctly refused with 400 VALIDATION_ERROR naming the declared columns, and ?id=eq.1 correctly filters on the real column. So 'pk' is special-cased into an accepted-but-unapplied parameter on the read path. Concretely: with 3 rows present, GET ?pk=eq.1 -> all 3 rows, GET ?id=eq.1 -> the one row. Why it matters: the API teaches callers that pk addresses a row (it does for PATCH/DELETE), so an agent that patches and then verifies with the same URL is told its change either did nothing or hit everything — and a delete verification reads as 'still there' only because the row is genuinely gone. That is the exact class of inconsistency that gets an autonomous caller to retry a write that already succeeded.", "acceptance_criteria": "Make read addressing agree with write addressing, and prove all three cases. (1) GET /api/ns/:ns/tables/:table?pk=eq.<v> returns exactly the row whose declared primary key equals v (or a documented 400 if the table declares no primary — not a silent full scan). (2) A falsifier in tests: with 3 rows, GET ?pk=eq.<one> returns 1 row while GET with no filter returns 3, and GET ?<pkcolumn>=eq.<one> returns the same 1 row (alias must not disagree with the column form). (3) An unknown filter column still refuses with VALIDATION_ERROR naming the declared columns (do not relax that to make the alias work). (4) PATCH and DELETE continue to accept ?pk=eq. and their responses are unchanged — prove with the existing suites plus one end-to-end run of declare -> insert 3 -> GET by pk -> PATCH by pk -> DELETE by pk -> GET by pk returns 0. Update the docs/spec (SUPA-3) to state the addressing rule once, so read and write verbs cannot drift again.", "updated_at": "2026-11-04T02:30:55Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "cb86d95abac1f12b5de7354a2c1d471a4b402b524a98fe14632956511d1322e9"}, "worker_status": "pending", "commit_hash": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "foreman_note": null}
